  "response": "Here's a Python function called `bank_rollback` that applies a list of transactions to an initial balance in sequence. If at any point the balance becomes negative (i.e., strictly less than 0), it stops immediately and returns the original balance with `False`. Otherwise, it returns the final balance with `True`:\n\n```python\ndef bank_rollback(balance, transactions):\n    \"\"\"\n    Applies transactions to a starting balance in order.\n    Returns (original_balance, False) if the balance becomes negative during calculation.\n    Returns (final_balance, True) if all transactions complete successfully.\n\n    Args:\n        balance (float or int): Starting account balance.\n        transactions (list of float/int): List of transaction amounts.\n\n    Returns:\n        tuple: (resulting_balance_or_original_balance, success_flag)\n    \"\"\"\n    current_balance = balance\n    \n    for transaction in transactions:\n        current_balance += transaction\n        \n        if current_balance < 0:\n            return (balance, False)\n    \n    return (current_balance, True)\n```\n\n### Example Usage:\n\n```python\nprint(bank_rollback(100, [50, -30, -200]))  # (100, False)\nprint(bank_rollback(100, [50, -30, -50]))   # (120, True)\nprint(bank_rollback(0, [-50]))              # (0, False)\nprint(bank_rollback(-50, [50, 20]))         # (-50, True)\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- Checks only if the balance becomes negative after each transaction (`< 0`), not at zero.\n- If an initial balance is already negative, it continues applying transactions unless a subsequent operation causes the balance to drop below zero again.\n- The function preserves the original balance in failure cases and ensures consistent behavior across edge inputs.",
